=== task: mcq medium
title: Why is popping the back O(n)?
prompt: A singly linked list keeps a `tail`, so appending is O(1). Why is removing the last node still O(n)?
* The node before the tail must become the new tail, and only a walk from the head can find it :: Right. A singly linked node doesn't know the node before it.
- Removing a node shifts the nodes after it :: Nothing shifts in a linked list; only links change.
- The tail has to be copied into a new node :: Nothing is copied: the node before the tail gets `next = None`.
- It isn't: with a tail it's O(1) :: The tail gives you the last node, but not the one before it, which is the one that changes.
hint: Which node's link changes when the last node goes?
hint: Can the tail tell you which node comes before it?
right: Removing the last node changes the second-to-last node's link, and finding that node takes a walk: O(n). A doubly linked list has it in `tail.prev`.
wrong: The second-to-last node has to link to None and become the tail. Only a walk from the head can find it in a singly linked list.

=== task: multi medium
title: Reversing in place
