* It makes no new nodes :: Right. It only changes links.
* The old head ends up last, linking to None :: Right. On the first pass its link is flipped to `prev`, which is None.
* It uses O(1) extra space :: Right. Three names, whatever the length.
* It returns `prev`, the old last node :: Right. When `cur` is None, `prev` is on the old last node.
- It takes O(n²) time :: Each node is visited once: O(n).
- It needs to know the length of the list first :: It walks until `cur` is None.
hint: What does the loop make, and how many times does it run?
hint: Four of them are true.
right: One pass, three names, every link flipped: O(n) time, O(1) extra space, and the old last node is the new head.
wrong: True: no new nodes, the old head ends up last, O(1) extra space, `prev` is returned. False: it's O(n) time, and it needs no length.

=== task: code medium
title: The middle node
prompt: Write `middle(head)` that returns the value of the middle node of a list that isn't empty. With an even number of nodes, return the second of the two middle nodes: the node at index n // 2.

For example, `middle(Node(1, Node(2, Node(3, Node(4)))))` returns 3.
starter:
